{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1786429124,"nonce":"s+VZjGn7yM58x4ikSEa66IRwQ9Y=","message":"Done, and the bound set is now B1-B19 (B18/B19 landed with e91be9e). Two files: decode/bounds_boundary_test.go for every bound decidable without a database, and test/integration/ingest_bounds_test.go for the same bounds seen from outside — HTTP status, the errs code in the RFC 9457 body, the ingest_rejections.reason rows, and the receipt counters. B17 chunking is proved with a chunkRecorder at the one cross-module port, every repository underneath still real on real Postgres, asserting chunk sizes {500},{500,1},{500,500,500,500} and one pg_current_xact_id() per chunk.\n\nNO OFF-BY-ONE FOUND. Every numeric bound is `\u003e Max…`, i.e. inclusive, consistently, and the DDL agrees.\n\nThree findings worth acting on:\n1. B17's CODE CONTRADICTS ITS OWN DOC COMMENT AND THE SPEC. applyChunks says 'a batch at or under ChunkThreshold is ONE transaction, which is the common case and the fast one'. It is not — the loop slices by ChunkSize unconditionally, so a 2000-alert batch runs FOUR transactions of 500. ChunkThreshold gates only the partial marking. SPEC §L.3.2's B17 row reads the same way the comment does. Three things to reconcile; the test asserts what the code does and says loudly which sentence is wrong.\n2. ⚠️ B18/B19 ARE UNREACHABLE THROUGH THE PUSH PATH. Accept writes the raw body verbatim into ingest_batches.payload JSONB and defers per-alert bounds to the worker, but Postgres jsonb cannot hold U+0000 (22P05) or invalid UTF-8 (22021) — the exact premise §L.3.2a rests on. So such a webhook fails the accept INSERT, is mapped by asBackpressure to 503 + Retry-After, and is retried by Alertmanager forever with NO ingest_rejections row — the opposite of what B18/B19 promise. SPEC §L.3.3 puts per-alert bounds at step 6, before the persist at step 9; the code's latency-driven deferral is what opens the gap. Reasoned from the code path plus documented Postgres behaviour, not executed. Filed separately.\n3. B11 (MaxAlertNameBytes) has no reason member of its own — it shares label_value_too_large with B5. And rejected_alerts on the 202 is always 0 even when alerts will be rejected, because per-alert bounds run in the worker; pinned so nobody 'fixes' it into a lie.","files":null}]}